      "id": "commentary",
      "html": "<h2>commentary</h2>  <p>This is an epitaph found on a stone plaque. The syntax of the epitaph is complex and has led to differing interpretations. Ferrara (1829, 342) believed that two brothers (Aphrodisias and Maryllas) and a sister (Theuda) had dedicated the tomb to their parents. Kaibel (ap. IG XIV 465) shared this view, but understood the names in the genitive case (in this case, Aphrodisias and Maryllas would be the daughters, Theudas the son), without explaining why the names of the dedicators would appear in this case while the names of the parents were not expressed. Franz (ap. CIG III 5698), followed by Manni Piraino (1973, 28), however, held a different opinion, interpreting the genitives as dependent on <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">τὸ μνημεῖὸν ἐστι</span>, thus effectively taking these names as those of the parents, while the names of the sons would not have been expressed. The sons would therefore have been born to a single father (Theudas), but to two different mothers (Aphrodisia and Marylla). Another possibility would be to understand the genitive as an expression of ownership: having built the tomb for their parents, the sons may have wished to claim that it was their exclusive property. <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Ἀφροδίσια</span> is a fairly common female name in Sicily (LGPN 3A p. 86), <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Θεῦδας</span> could be an epichoric form for <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Θεόδωρος</span> (on which, see Manni Piraino 1973, 28) and <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Μαρύλλας</span> is attested in another inscription from Catania (IG 14.491 = <a href=\"https://sicily.classics.ox.ac.uk/inscription/ISic000361\" target=\"_blank\">ISic000361</a>).</p>  "
